INT. MARS TRANSIT STATION, MAINTENANCE BAY - SOL 412

A low gray room lit by rack lights. A rover on a cradle, its wheel hub stripped. Tools laid out on a magnetic table. Dust on every surface, the red fine-grained dust that gets everywhere.

TECH ADAEZE CHUKWUDI-VANCE (30s) is on her back under the rover. Her boots stick out. Across the bay, DR. CASIMIR NAKASHIMA-BRIGGS (50s) taps at a console.

A stranger stands in the airlock hatch: a man in a dusty suit, helmet under one arm, a clipboard in his other hand.

ADAEZE
(from under the rover)
Cas, hand me the 10-mil.

CASIMIR
Which one's the 10?

ADAEZE
The one that isn't the 12.

The stranger steps forward.

SURVEYOR
Excuse me. I'm looking for the station supervisor.

Adaeze rolls out. Casimir turns.

CASIMIR
That would be me. And you are?

SURVEYOR
Colin Maddox-Reyes. Surveyor, Earth Authority.

ADAEZE
Surveyor, huh? You came a long way for a clipboard.

COLIN
(smiling)
It's a good clipboard.

The intercom crackles.

ORBITER (V.O.)
(over intercom)
Station, Orbiter. We've got an inbound dust front. Thirty minutes.

CASIMIR
Copy, Orbiter.

ADAEZE
Colin, you picked a lovely day.

COLIN
Just my luck, Adaeze.

ADAEZE
(squinting)
I didn't tell you my name.

Colin's smile does not change. The clipboard clatters to the floor. His other hand comes up holding a very small, very precise pistol.
